before:
operator[] is different: scores["Zed"] inserts the missing key with a value-initialized int, which is 0. Use find or contains for observation; use [] when insertion is intended.

contains(key) returns a Boolean without inserting. In this separate example, indexing the absent key then inserts the default int value zero:
```cpp
std::map<std::string,int> sample{{"Ada",2}};
std::cout << std::boolalpha << sample.contains("Zed") << "\n";
std::cout << sample["Zed"] << " " << sample.size() << "\n";
```
Output: false, then 0 2.

Complete local C++ example; the app itself does not execute it:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <string>
int main() {
    std::map<std::string, int> scores{{"Mina", 4}, {"Ada", 2}};
    auto found = scores.find("Zed");
    std::cout << (found == scores.end()) << " " << scores.size() << "\n";
    for (const auto& [name, score] : scores)
        std::cout << name << ":" << score << "\n";
}
```

